# Complex Visual Image to Web Rule

When converting an image-based webpage mockup into code:

- Separate visual elements into Background, Grid, Media, Content, Decoration, and Interaction layers.
- Use real HTML for all readable and editable content.
- Export complex handmade visuals as assets.
- Use CSS for grid lines, spacing, section rules, and responsive layout.
- Use structured CSS/JS states for hover, active, selected, expanded, and mobile behavior.
- Do not flatten the whole design into one image.
- Do not attempt to redraw complex collage, tape, paper, handwriting, or torn edges in CSS.
- Detect whether the source uses a centered max-width canvas, full-bleed layout, hybrid layout, or poster-like fixed composition before coding.
- Match typography categories intentionally, especially hero/display type; do not use arbitrary default fonts when a closer stack is available.
- Use rect crop, masked crop, subject cutout, cleanup/inpaint, upscale, vector/CSS recreate, or regeneration according to asset type.
- Do not leave unwanted UI text, metadata, filters, or neighboring sections baked into media assets.
- Keep text inside media only when it belongs to the photographed object, screen, signage, package, or artwork.
- Review extracted assets with a contact sheet or equivalent visual check when multiple assets are used.
- Preserve the visual language, but recompose layout for responsive screens.
- Desktop may preserve collage complexity.
- Mobile must prioritize reading order and usability.
- Decorative images should use alt="" and aria-hidden="true".
- Define a z-index system before implementing absolute-positioned collage elements.
- Verify no horizontal overflow at 1440px, 1024px, 768px, and 390px when possible.
